WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:06.790
So here you can see we have a very thin foil
sample of dysprosium. Again it is from the

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:09.420
bottom
end of the periodic table, very, very reactive.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.300
Stored very, very carefully as a foil. Now
if we

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.139
open, very carefully, we might see if the
metal is

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:17.150
still there.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:23.520
Ok so dysprosium is used in nuclear fuel rods
because it is very good at capturing neutrons,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.970
which means that you can modulate how hot
a

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.050
nuclear reaction is getting which is why it
is used

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.070
in powerstations to prevent the reactions
from

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:32.230
running away out of control.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:37.090
So take off all this foil and then we can
withdraw

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:46.609
the sample. So it is in this really nice paper.
So is

00:00:46.609 --> 00:00:50.670
the sample still there? Oh beautiful, here
we see

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:58.350
this wonderful sample of dysprosium.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:06.780
If you mix dysprosium with cadmium and sulphur
it can be used in devices which produce infrared

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.000
beams. Now chemists use infrared beams quite
a

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.350
lot because when you irradiate a sample, a
compound with infrared then certain

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.600
absorbencies will occur which are specific
to

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:26.740
stretching or bending modes which the molecule
will do. This is a way of, therefore, scanning

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.689
molecules and getting information about their
composition and structure.

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:31.689
Beautiful foil.

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:33.200
Do you happen to know anything about this
element?

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.229
Not a thing, that’s Steve’s domain.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:37.049
Yeah!

